This looks like a phonemic (phonological) similarity effect in short-term memory, where similar-sounding items are recalled worse. The reported F(1,15) = 214.17 is very large, and the phonological similarity effect is among the most robust findings in cognitive psychology. Replications should succeed even with a modest sample.

The "within-subject: no" label is probably a coding quirk, since the F test with 15 df implies a within-subject design. Overall the effect is very strong, so the replication probability is high. The main risks are a different design, differences in the replication procedure, or a mismatch in the specific claim.

