This looks like the linguistic intergroup bias (LIB) inference study, probably Porter & Rios or a similar paper in Psychological Science. The claim is that people infer shared group membership from language use. The original effect is moderate (d = 0.51, p about .003), with N = 133.

Replications of this kind of study tend to succeed when the manipulation is direct and the effect is a main effect. The within-subject or nested design adds some uncertainty. I don't recall a specific replication outcome. A replication would likely use a larger sample, which helps with power even if the true effect is somewhat smaller than the original estimate. I put the probability around 0.6.

