This appears to come from Levine et al. (2020), "The logic of universalization guides moral judgment" (PNAS). The effect is large (76% vs 44%), the sample is 608, and the design is a simple between-subjects vignette comparison. Moral-judgment vignette effects of this size usually replicate, and the study was likely included in a replication project such as SCORE. Replication risks are the usual ones: vignette wording and sample differences.

I don't recall the specific replication outcome.

