This is Zhong & Liljenquist (2006), "Washing away your sins," Science. Study 2 had a small sample (N=27) and p = .01. Replications of the Macbeth effect have mostly failed. Earlier, Earp et al. (2014) failed to replicate Study 2-type effects, and the Many Labs-style replication in the Reproducibility Project and the Camerer et al. replication of social science Science papers covered other studies. I don't recall a specific outcome for this claim. The effect size is large for the sample (F = 6.99, so r ≈ 0.47), which makes it likely inflated. Priors are low given the failed replications of related cleansing effects.

Note that this claim might have come from a replication project, such as the SCORE project. Even so, the base rate is low for a small-N priming effect.

