This is Zhong & Liljenquist (2006), Study 4, "Washing away your sins." Cleansing was done with antiseptic wipes, and the outcome was volunteering for a graduate student's study. The effect was reported with N=45 and p=.025.

Replication evidence is poor. Earlier Many Labs-type and other replication attempts of the Macbeth effect (Earp et al. 2014) failed to reproduce the original cleansing effects. Other replications of the Schnall cleansing effect also failed. This specific study was likely replicated in the Camerer et al. 2018 project (Science), which targeted Zhong & Liljenquist? No, I'm not sure it was. It may have been included in a different replication effort, such as the Many Labs or a Registered Replication Report. Either way, the small sample, the marginal p-value, and the priming-like mechanism all point toward a low chance of replication.


