This looks like Kidd & Castano-related work: a study of fiction exposure (the Author Recognition Test) and Reading the Mind in the Eyes Test (RMET) scores. The original r = .36 with N = 80 and p = .0003 is fairly strong. Related literature, including Mar et al. 2006 and Panero et al. 2016, found that the ART correlates with RMET. A replication would likely have a larger sample, and the correlation between ART and RMET tends to be about .2-.3. Vocabulary and education confound the relationship, but the claim is only a simple correlation. Panero et al. (2016) ran a direct replication of the Kidd & Castano effect and found no experimental effect, though the ART–RMET correlation did appear in some of the data. Overall, a correlation of this size usually replicates with adequate power.


