This claim looks like it comes from Zhang & Schwarz or Janiszewski/Uy work on scale unit width and anchoring, where motivation to adjust moderates the effect. I don't specifically recall a replication of it.

The reported F(1, 55) = 6.88 corresponds to p ≈ .011, so the evidence is moderate. The sample is small (59) and the effect is an interaction, with a small w2 of .02. Interactions with small effect sizes and small samples tend to replicate at lower rates, so I estimate roughly 35-40%.

