This looks like a study from Systematic Review of experimental philosophy, perhaps Sytsma & Machery (2010), "Two conceptions of subjective experience." They found that people were more willing to ascribe pain to a robot than to ascribe seeing red. The claim compares folk ascription of seeing red with feeling pain, probably for a robot, and the difference was large. The original p < .001 with N = 111 and r ≈ 0.4. The effect has been replicated in various forms, including in the Many Labs-style xphi replicability project (Cova et al. 2018), which replicated many xphi studies at a high rate. Sytsma & Machery was probably among those replicated (I believe "Two conceptions of subjective experience" was replicated). The between-subjects design and the sizeable effect favor success.


