This looks like the Garner-and-London study, a difference-in-differences or interrupted time series design that uses Metropolitan Police Public Attitudes Survey data. The sample of 47 is probably the number of time periods (months or weeks) rather than the number of individuals. Black Londoners are a small subsample, so the series is noisy, and a single event is a thin basis for a causal claim. The p-value of .004 is fairly strong, but a design with few time points and an uncertain mechanism is fragile.

A replication would likely re-analyze the same data, or use new data covering the same event. If it used the same data, the result would probably replicate. If it used new data, success is less likely. Overall, the odds are slightly below even.

