This looks like a study on masculine generics versus gender-fair alternatives, possibly Stahlberg and Sczesny (2001) or a related German study on mental representations of women. It could also be Gabriel, Gygax and colleagues. I can't identify the specific study.

The original had n=90 and p<.01, so the evidence is moderate. A planned contrast of masculine generics against two alternatives is plausible, since masculine generics reliably reduce the representation of women. The effect is fairly robust in the literature, and replications of this kind of language effect tend to succeed when the sample is adequate. Some uncertainty remains because of the small original sample and possible differences in language or materials.


